<section class="section-tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head-center">
      <div class="eyebrow">Rented vs. Owned</div>
      <h2>Paid Traffic Stops When You Stop Paying. Organic Assets Don't.</h2>
      <p class="lede center">This isn't ads-bad-SEO-good. Paid traffic is fast, testable, and genuinely useful for validating an offer. Organic search asks for a different kind of investment: mostly your time. A well-built product page or article keeps earning you visits long after you stopped actively working on it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rvo-scroll">
      <table class="rvo-table">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Paid Traffic (rented)</th><th>Organic Search (owned)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Speed to first customer</strong></td><td>Fast (days)</td><td class="owned">Slow (weeks to months)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cost structure</strong></td><td>Ongoing, per click/impression</td><td class="owned">Upfront time/content investment, then low marginal cost</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Needs testing before it's efficient</strong></td><td>Yes, on creatives, audiences, and offers</td><td class="owned">Less "testing," more "building"</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Stops working if you stop</strong></td><td>Immediately</td><td class="owned">Gradually. Content and structure keep working for a while.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Validating offers, launching fast, a specific promotion</td><td class="owned">Long-term discoverability, buyers who research before buying</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-note center" style="margin-top:18px;">No invented ad-cost figures here. The workbook's Section 2 gives you a calculator to work out your own numbers instead.</p>
  </div>
</section>

